{
 "id": "elbow-bearing-bracket-sls",
 "brief": "The elbow bearing bracket on my the desk arm, SLS PA12. It bolts flat to the upper arm's side\nplate (z = 0) with four M4 socket-head screws along Z at (+/-22, +/-16), through 4.5 mm holes in a\n5 mm thick foot (z = 0 to 5); the heads bear on top of the foot, so each needs a flat 9 mm seat at\nz = 5 and a 9 mm column clear straight up above it for the hex key. The bracket holds two 608\nbearings back to back for the elbow shaft: their axis is along Y through (0, 0, 42), the seat is\n22 mm diameter from y = -7 to 7 with at least 4 mm of wall round it, the bearings press in from\nthe -Y side (keep the 22 mm bore open out that way), and the 8 mm shaft runs right through along Y\n(10 mm clearance). Everything fits in x = -32 to 32, y = -22 to 22, z = 0 to 60. Loads on the\nbearings: up to 150 N radial from the forearm, in any direction perpendicular to the shaft; 60 N\nof axial thrust along +Y; and a 2.5 N.m tipping moment about +X from the forearm hanging off the\nshaft. Safety factor 2, and under 16 g.",
